<https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/entries/bertalanffy-1972-history-status>
    a owl:Ontology ;
    owl:imports <https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/atlas-core> ;
    rdfs:label "Atlas entry — Bertalanffy 1972, The History and Status of General Systems Theory"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Entry 004. The founder of the tradition, entered from a journal article rather
than the 1968 book — the first non-book bearer in the catalogue. The reason is transcription
discipline, recorded in the entry's apparatus."""@en .


##############################################################################
# Bearer — the first journal article in the catalogue
##############################################################################

bearer:bertalanffy-1972-amj
    a cco:ont00000253 ;   # Information Bearing Entity
    rdfs:label "Von Bertalanffy, L. — The History and Status of General Systems Theory, Academy of Management Journal 15(4) (1972)"@en ;
    dcterms:creator "Ludwig von Bertalanffy" ;
    atlas:authoredBy author:ludwig-von-bertalanffy ;
    dcterms:date "1972"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dcterms:identifier "https://www.jstor.org/stable/255139" ;
    dcterms:identifier "doi:10.2307/255139" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Academy of Management Journal 15(4), December 1972, pp. 407–426. Special issue: General Systems Theory." .


##############################################################################
# Entry 004 — Bertalanffy's general definition
##############################################################################

entry:bertalanffy-1972-set-in-interrelation
    a atlas:FormalSystemDefinition ;
    rdfs:label "Bertalanffy (1972), The History and Status of General Systems Theory, p. 417"@en ;

    atlas:statedIn bearer:bertalanffy-1972-amj ;
    atlas:sourceLocation "p. 417" ;

    atlas:verbatim """A system may be defined as a set of elements standing in interrelation among themselves and with the environment. This can be expressed mathematically in different ways.""" ;

    # Presentation spans — exact substrings of the verbatim, enforced by the build.
    # The definition leads; the author's own hedge about formalisation follows it,
    # which is the same shape as the other entries' formula-then-gloss.
    atlas:displayForm "A system may be defined as a set of elements standing in interrelation among themselves and with the environment." ;
    atlas:displayContext "This can be expressed mathematically in different ways." ;

    # Conservative: only terms the definitional sentence itself uses. "element" is a
    # new primitive — no existing row fits it, and collapsing it onto `thing` would
    # assert a synonymy across traditions that the scheme's own scope note denies.
    atlas:invokesPrimitive prim:element ,
                           prim:set ,
                           prim:relation ,
                           prim:environment ;

    # No cases. The organism ruling recorded here until 2026-08-07 was from the
    # 1968 book, parked on this entry while it was the only Bertalanffy entry;
    # it now lives with its own bearer on entry 005 (bertalanffy-1968-gst.ttl).
    # The 1972 article itself never rules on a concrete object.

    # The 1972 shape, NOT the 1968 one. The verbatim says "and with the
    # environment", which is a third position and a second arrow; pointing this
    # entry at BertalanffyShape (two positions) would have claimed a formalisation
    # that omits the clause the 1972 restatement exists to add.
    atlas:formalisedAs "Systems/Category/ShapeBertalanffy.lean#Bertalanffy72Shape" ;

    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:HVP ;
    atlas:encodedBy "Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-08-05"^^xsd:date ;

    rdfs:comment """HVP: the page was read against this transcription on 2026-08-05, including
the two things a paraphrase would smooth away — that it prints "may be defined" rather than
"can be", and that "and with the environment" is really there. The second is the clause that
distinguishes this definition from the 1968 book's, so the whole entry turns on it.

WHY THE 1972 ARTICLE AND NOT THE 1968 BOOK. The book is the canonical
citation, and its ch. 3 (p. 55) gives the same definition verbally — "a complex of interacting
elements" — followed by the differential-equation formalisation, eq. (3.1). The only copy of
the book available to this encoding is an OCR whose ligatures are systematically damaged and
in which **equation (3.1) did not survive at all**. Transcribing a verbatim from it would
violate the catalogue's first discipline. The 1972 article restates the definition in a text
with clean provenance, so it is entered first. The 1968 entry remains wanted, and the pair is
worth having: the definitions differ.

WHAT THE REVISION CHANGED. 1968: "a complex of interacting elements." 1972: "a set of elements
standing in interrelation among themselves AND WITH THE ENVIRONMENT." The environment becomes
constitutive of the definition rather than a later distinction. Encoding both would make that
revision machine-checkable at the shape level — the 1972 shape carries an arrow the 1968 shape
does not.

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY OUT OF THE VERBATIM. The differential-equation material sits on the
following page (p. 418, "a set of n measures, called state variables... a set of n
simultaneous, first-order differential equations"), and its equation is a page image in this
scan, not extractable text. Including that sentence would end the verbatim on a colon pointing
at something the catalogue does not hold. It is a candidate for a second entry from the same
bearer, in the way Bunge's CES triple and Definition 1.1 are two entries from one book.

THE AUTHOR'S OWN HEDGE is inside the verbatim, not paraphrased around it: "This can be
expressed mathematically in different ways." On the same page he names Mesarović, Maccia,
Ashby and Klir as fellow axiomatisers — the founder pointing at two other entries in this
catalogue.""" ;

    atlas:authorCaveat "This can be expressed mathematically in different ways." .


##############################################################################
# Cases — none. The organism ruling moved to entry 005 (bertalanffy-1968-gst.ttl)
# on 2026-08-07, with the move recorded on the case itself.
##############################################################################
